{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T15:01:30.592Z","ordinal":36376,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0200a-63d8-7b21-adef-c3850aecebf7","turn_id":"01a08bd5-ec9a-7d00-af66-fe43a1be1f11","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_0873a68aa02aff8f016aa2c63efb9c81989f32fca483a9b591","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"This is not a fact-review screen. It validates the deterministic filter that decides which conversation turns the fact extractor is allowed to see.\n\nWhy it matters: candidate mode gets its speedup by discarding about 99.8% of transcript input. If that filter drops a real standing rule or decision, no model can recover it later.\n\nYour labels establish ground truth:\n\n- Selected statement + “No” = false positive.\n- Filtered statement + “Yes” = missed durable knowledge.\n- “Did the user say/approve it?” tests authority.\n- “Safe evidence?” catches one-off tasks, pasted text, tool output, and review speculation.\n\nSo the screenshot item exposes a real selector defect: it matched “don’t” and selected a one-off planning request. Your `No / Yes / No` records that failure.\n\nThe 20-item calibration batch is meant to confirm that the rubric makes sense before asking you to continue. After human labels exist, we can calculate whether candidate mode meets the Phase 2 recall and precision gates. Copilot, Cursor, and Claude are supporting opinions; they cannot supply the ground truth.\n\nIf we skip this human labeling, candidate mode can remain experimental, but we cannot honestly claim that its large performance gain preserves knowledge quality."}],"phase":"final_answer"},"started_at_ms":1789052479104,"completed_at_ms":1789052490592}}
