io/entire-graph/pull/75 (stacked on #73 → #72). ## Codex review: we agree on the class, and it corrected me twice Codex ran ~285k tokens over the repo and used `entire why` to pull commit provenance. It agreed the defect class is real, and pushed back on my framing in two ways I'd already half-corrected — plus found the biggest issue. **Where it corrected me:** most of the \"capabilities drift\" I measured is intentional. `heuristic_relation_types` is a separate advertised field, so the honest comparison is `per-language ∪ global heuristics`, not the language matrix alone. It also found that `ooRelationSupport` *gates emission* for `CONFIGURES`/`RESOURCE_DEPENDS_ON` on IaC languages — a legitimate allowlist any fix must preserve. **Where it strengthened me:** existing tests at `provider_test.go:12195` and `:12307` *require* R and Julia to emit `CALLS`, while `capabilities` advertises only `CONTAINS`/`DEFINES` for both. The test suite and the capability report contradict each other in-tree. ## The finding that matters most — Codex's, verified by me Codex flagged `skipFastProfilePerSymbolScan` from source. It couldn't run tests in its sandbox, so I measured it: | repo | `--profile full` | `--profile fast` | |---|---|---| | cargo (Rust) | 21,509 CALLS | **14** | | cli-cli (Go, allowlisted) | 20,800 CALLS | 3,361 | **22 of 30 call-capable languages** — Rust, C, C++, Kotlin, Swift, Scala, Zig, Dart, Elixir, Haskell, Bash, Lua, Perl, OCaml, Erlang, Groovy, SQL and more…\nuser: Implement the whole plan. Test and verify that your changes do not lead to regressions.\nassistant: This is substantial. Let me track it and work through methodically on a new stacked branch.\n--- panel-v1:05c7e254d692ccbb072d29c00e976f10b9fea7c00d7e568e0122347d0ffd610a\nassistant: Launched. Everything running: ## What's happening now **Subagent orchestrating the full snapshot measurement** — 20 instances (+3 Python) × 3 fresh arms, sonnet: - **baseline** (no tool) → `snap20base` - **cmm** → `snap20cmm` - **eg best config** → `snap20` = frozen binary (`entire-graph-snap20` = PR #54 stack: test-demote + vocab-bridge + centrality) + `--profile full` + tiered output + search-only prompt Deliverable: per-instance table, per-language rollup, weighted+unweighted aggregate, winner counts → `results/SNAP20-REPORT.md`. ETA ~2-4h (≈70 sonnet sessions). Notifies when done. **Snapshot frozen** (`SNAPSHOT-20RUN.md`, commit `ce34583`): binary sha256, eg commit `2a10cc8`, tool hashes, exact env — so when we change code next, this measurement stays the fixed reference point. ## To your question — \"only rust and java left?\" Close but not exact. By median, eg is **ahead on 10 of 11**; the precise state: - **Clear wins (7):** Python, Go, JS, C, PHP, Ruby, C++ - **Thin edge, need firming (3):** Java (+0.3/+1.8), docusaurus (+0.2), immutable (+1.0 but coin-flip variance) - **Median loss (1):** Rust/ripgrep — and the new finding matters here: **cmm's own search ALSO can't find the ripgrep fix** (checked its top-12: same regex-layer hits, gold absent). cmm wins it on terseness/fewer-tokens-per-turn, not retrieval. So \"if cmm can do it\" — it can't, retrieval-wise; its edge is output efficiency, which is exactly what our tiered output targets. That's the beatable surface. The fresh 20-run gives the definitive cur…\nuser: GOAL: Beat codebase-memory-mcp (CMM) on the SWE-bench-Multilingual POLYGLOT AGGREGATE (weighted total-token savings; graphmark/agentic-swebench; sonnet `claude -p`). Do NOT restart — build on the measured state below. READ FIRST (verify, don't re-litigate): $SP/graphmark/agentic-swebench/BEAT-CMM-VERDICT.md + CMM-TECHNIQUE-PLAN.md (the LSP-port design) + [[beat-cmm-variance-verdict]]. CURRENT MEASURED STANDING: - eg ALREADY BEATS cmm per-language: Python -71.4% vs -64.0% (+7.4pt, 3x, 3/3 sympy/ requests/astropy; config = branch fb7 `graph-monorepo-feedback-fixes` + search-only tool profile) and C (-35% vs -18%); ties C++/JS. - eg LOSES Rust (+80% vs -7%), PHP, Java, Go — all TYPE-RESOLUTION-heavy; tree-sitter can't match cmm's Hybrid-LSP. - Polyglot AGGREGATE: cmm still wins (loser-lang weight). - entire-graph PR #47 MERGED (8744902): search-ranking fixes. Partial 4-instance re-measure flipped carbon-PHP (+40.6 vs +37.6) + gin-Go (+57 vs +43) to WINS — positive signal, NOT a full scoreboard. - CHEAP LEVERS ARE DRY (measured dead): further search-ranking, test/doc demotion, --profile fast/full (search is syntax-only, ignores CALLS edges), locations-only, full-body. Recall probes: laravel/faker/jekyll/docusaurus gold-rank 1-4 already — their loss is agent turn-count, not retrieval; no ranking change flips them. THE REAL LEVER (the wall): the aggregate is blocked on LSP-GRADE TYPE/CALL RESOLUTION for Rust/PHP/Java/Go (cmm's moat). Add real type/import resolution to entire-graph's snapshot per CMM-TECHNIQUE-PLAN.md. MAJOR architectural build, not a lever. That is the work. HARD CONSTRAINTS: - NO REGRESSION on winners (Python, C) — re-measure them as guards every round. - 3x+ multi-run per repo per config. Variance ±40-90pt/repo, aggregate ±16pt; single-run = noise. A win = eg's 3x aggregate mean beats cmm's by MORE than the band. - INTEGRITY: arms differ ONLY by the tool; faithful mini-swe-agent baseline, never inflated; no cherry-picking / rerun-only-failures / pass@k-as-pass@1 / tune-to-target. If eg still loses the aggregate, REPORT the honest per-language scoreboard + residual gap — a per-language win (Python/C + post-#47 PHP/Go) with \"aggregate needs LSP\" is a VALID outcome. Do NOT fabricate. - Known bug: `entire-graph search --max-snippet-lines 1` CRASHES (search.go:~3092); harness uses 2/6. Fix or avoid. RUN AS A GRAPH LOOP (our plugin `entire loop`, installed, best-in-class): Phase 0 — FULL 3x scoreboard: eg-merged (PR#47, /tmp/graphmark-bin/entire-graph-merged) vs cmm, 3x each, full 20-repo set, score with tools/dt_score.py. The #47 re-measure was only 4 instances — get the REAL current aggregate gap + per-language board. Truth baseline. Phase 1..N — improve loop: entire loop \"Close the polyglot-aggregate gap vs CMM by adding LSP-grade type/call resolution for Rust/PHP/Java/Go per CMM-TECHNIQUE-PLAN.md, WITHOUT regressing the Python/C wins. <paste the measured standing + constraints above>\" \\ --repo <entire-graph checkout> \\ --allow-mutating-build --plan-mode dynamic --jobs 3 --max-rounds <N> \\ --measure-cmd '<graphmark 3x runner: build+install the modified entire-graph FROM the clone, run agentic-swebench 3x cmm-vs-eg on the multilingual set, emit JSON {\"agg_gap_pt\":<eg_minus_cmm_weighted>,\"python_gap_pt\":..,\"c_gap_pt\":.., \"rust_gap_pt\":..,\"php_gap_pt\":..,\"java_gap_pt\":..,\"go_gap_pt\":..}>' \\ --converge-metric agg_gap_pt --converge-at <margin over the ±16pt band> Per round: research diagnoses the losing lang's resolution gap from graphmark output; the mutating build seat implements the LSP/resolver change in an isolated clone; the measure-cmd builds+installs from that clone + runs graphmark 3x -> agg_gap_pt + per-lang guards; converge when eg's 3x aggregate beats cmm by > the band with NO winner regression. The measure-cmd is a multi-hour, docker-backed, multi-run job -> set a LONG measure timeout, budget the cost; it measures the BUILD CLONE (the improved graph). Winning changes are proposed diffs from the clone (apply the audited winner; the plugin never auto-merges/installs). DELIVERABLE: the honest 3x scoreboard (per-language + weighted aggregate, eg vs cmm, mean ± band), the LSP-resolution code that moved the losers, no-winner-regression proof, and a one-line verdict: aggregate win (survived 3x + audit) OR per-language win + residual LSP gap. No fabricated wins.\nassistant: Goal registered — but note: **/goal failed to set** (\"limited to 4000 characters, got 4570\") — the Stop-hook enforcement is NOT active. I'm treating the text as my standing instruction anyway; if you want the hook enforcing it, re-run `/goal` with a trimmed version, e.g.: `beat cmm on the polyglot weighted aggregate (3x mean, > ±16pt band), no winner regression, no fabrication; Phase 0 full 3x scoreboard first, then LSP/type-resolution rounds via entire loop`. Three corrections to the goal's snapshot (it's from this morning's memory; verified against the verdict doc — much has landed since): - \"loses PHP/Java/Go, cheap levers dry, search ignores CALLS\" → **stale**. Since then: PR #54 (test-demote −12 + gated CALLS-bridge + **centrality**) + tiered output + `--profile full` harness. **PHP + Ruby + C++ + docusaurus converted; Go won; Java parity-edge; only Rust median-loss + immutable variance.** - Best binary is **snap20** (PR #54 stack), not `entire-graph-merged` (#47) — Phase 0 must measure the current best, else we burn ~$100 measuring a superseded state. - **Phase 0 is already in flight**: the snap20 subagent is running the full 20+3 × 3 arms (r1). Goal needs 3× → I'll extend r2/r3 when r1 lands. Key implication: the aggregate may ALREADY flip — last aggregate (−41.4 vs −44.3, ~3pt gap) predates all four converting levers, and nlohmann alone swung from cmm-thrash (−44.8%) to eg +37.7%. **Phase 0 decides whether the LSP build is even needed.** Checking progress + prepping the goal's tooling now:\n--- panel-v1:08dc5b54f7a98f480b5c109563ecde2e95e6a6669e1c50e8a992599bf129ae25\nuser: Review Entire trails 3, 6, and 10. Update base branches as needed, fix errors, and make sure the trails are mergable.\nassistant: I’ll inspect Entire’s trail workflow first, then check trails 3, 6, and 10 against their current base branches, update them where necessary, run the repository checks, and fix any merge or test failures I find.\n--- panel-v1:d2f46e659e5b9afe533aadfb5a5903b8d4f1597ffefa0776362330cecc6fcc77\nassistant: **Best repo on Haiku v9: `micropython-15898` at −71%** — greps 14 → 1, turns 38 → 11, output 11,199 → 3,813. The top 12: | instance | lang | usd | greps | turns | output | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | **micropython-15898** | c | **−71%** | 14→1 | 38→11 | 11.2k→3.8k | | fastlane-19765 | ruby | −68% | 4→0 | 13→5 | 11.4k→1.7k | | laravel-48636 | php | −61% | 10→2 | 26→12 | 10.8k→4.5k | | immutable-js-2006 | js | −57% | 10→3 | 35→23 | 18.4k→9.0k | | valkey-1499 | c | −56% | 31→4 | 51→18 | 9.4k→9.8k | | carbon-3098 | php | −51% | 17→2 | 35→28 | 24.1k→9.1k | | faker-2970 | ruby | −50% | 8→7 | 34→24 | 16.6k→9.6k | | nushell-13246 | rust | −49% | 17→6 | 27→14 | 10.2k→4.6k | **Every one of the top 12 has the same signature: greps collapse and output falls with them.** micropython 14→1, valkey 31→4, hugo-12562 28→6. And notably almost all of them used just **`search1` or `search2`** — one or two calls, no `def`, no `callers`. The three worst invert it exactly: `axum` +97% (greps 9→**16**, output 4.8k→10.5k), `fmt` +44% (13→**17**), `scikit-learn` +33% (9→**11**). Greps go *up*, output goes up. Worth noting: **`micropython` is the best repo on Haiku (−71%) and the third-worst on Sonnet (+164%)** — same instance, same tool, same payload. On Haiku it eliminated 13 greps; on Sonnet the baseline only grepped 4 times, so there was nothing to eliminate and Sonnet's reasoning ballooned instead. That single instance is the clearest illustration of why the tool's value is entirely a function of how much the untooled agent would hav…\nuser: alright now make a cocnreate sonnet plan, don't rush\nassistant: Let me read the existing plan file first, then research properly before writing.\n--- panel-v1:e43fa08316d07671d67198cb52112626065d3a52d067c2ecbdf13e171352f676\nassistant: All three inline threads on #105 are resolved — including Cursor's, which now shows `resolved=true outdated=true` with my reply attached, since `5a311c3` changed the line it was anchored to. What's still visible is Cursor's **review summary** from 19:33 — the body that says \"Bugbot has reviewed your changes and found 1 potential issue.\" That's a top-level review, not a thread, so there's nothing to resolve; GitHub keeps it in the timeline permanently and it will keep advertising an issue that no longer exists. Its check on the PR passed, and its finding is fixed. Two ways to make the timeline say so, if the stale summary is what's bothering you: - Post `bugbot run` again — Bugbot re-reviews `5a311c3` and, assuming it now finds nothing, adds a fresh \"found no new issues\" summary below the old one. - Leave it; the resolved thread already records the fix, and the old summary is pinned to a commit that's no longer the head. I'd re-run it — it's cheap, and it gets the PR to a state where the newest bot verdict is the accurate one for anyone reading later. Want me to?\nuser: Yes\nassistant: Re-run triggered on #105 — [`bugbot run`](https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/pull/105#issuecomment-5305072125), now against `5a311c3` rather than the original `a3f5e21`. Polling for the fresh verdict. If it comes back clean, #105 ends with an accurate newest summary, three resolved threads, and 9 green checks — ready for its author to merge.\n--- panel-v1:ee0d466a070888a73c5a62a92c825b3a38a01a7cde20f4dc836811f8e2171fd6\nassistant: **Definitive plan is in `devenv/DESIGN.md` (iteration 7). Verdict: reachable, −35.0%, two changes.** You were right that the graph should save tokens, and the turn leg proves it: **clamped eg collapses the session −33.6%, CI [−49.2, −14.3] — excludes zero.** It finds the code. The leak is that it returns 27% of that win through **+38.7% new-tokens-per-turn**. **Mechanism, and it's why both my previous answers were wrong:** the payload is a **fixed per-session injection**, so its per-turn share scales *inversely* with session length — | cell | eg turns | marginal $/turn vs baseline | new-tok/turn vs baseline | |---|---|---|---| | cfg8 unclamped | 38.60 | **−3.1% (cheaper)** | +12% | | clamped | 15.28 | +20.3% | **+38.7%** | Iteration 5 assumed the premium *falls* on shorter sessions — backwards. Iteration 6 read the raw +29.7% usd/turn as irreducible payload cost, when much of it is the fixed-prefix length artifact (marginal rate shows eg **cheaper** at 39 turns). **Clamp and diet must move together** — clamping alone self-cancels, which is exactly the −33.6% turns → −15.6% usd leak. **Exact target:** | | now | needed | |---|---|---| | new tokens/turn | 2,162 | **≤1,527** → −31.2% | | turns | 15.28 | **≤14.4** → **−35.0%** | **W-A** k10 diet — measured free: −46.3% payload bytes at *identical* recall (23/30) and *identical* body count (2.60); plus drop the bodyless tail (73.6% of entries carry no body). **W-B** the 1.23 already-indexed-but-unranked reads → ~0.9 turns; a ranking change, not an indexing build-out.…\nuser: ok then implment it now and aduit and then make pr with veyrhting on entire grpahand grpahmrk so mains can be update to date; don't run ebchmarks tho; also cursor agnet migth have changed some stuff accoutnfor that too\nassistant: First, current state of both repos including anything Cursor changed:\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"--- panel-v1:05488f0a963ed54dd8ba7db2a59e62b55671410e5c1d1f6b69de3772b26e5660\nassistant: Pushed. **Draft PR #75**: https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/pull/75 (stacked on #73 → #72). ## Codex review: we agree on the class, and it corrected me twice Codex ran ~285k tokens over the repo and used `entire why` to pull commit provenance. It agreed the defect class is real, and pushed back on my framing in two ways I'd already half-corrected — plus found the biggest issue. **Where it corrected me:** most of the \"capabilities drift\" I measured is intentional. `heuristic_relation_types` is a separate advertised field, so the honest comparison is `per-language ∪ global heuristics`, not the language matrix alone. It also found that `ooRelationSupport` *gates emission* for `CONFIGURES`/`RESOURCE_DEPENDS_ON` on IaC languages — a legitimate allowlist any fix must preserve. **Where it strengthened me:** existing tests at `provider_test.go:12195` and `:12307` *require* R and Julia to emit `CALLS`, while `capabilities` ad